## Side by side

| | Props | State |
|---|-------|-------|
| **Comes from** | Parent | The component itself |
| **Mutable inside** | No — read only | Yes, via the setter |
| **Triggers re-render** | When the parent re-renders with new values | Yes, when set to a new value |
| **Analogy** | Function arguments | A variable that survives between calls |
| **Initial value** | Given by the caller | `useState(initial)` |

---

## In code

```jsx
function Counter({ step, label }) {   // ← props, read-only
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);  // ← state, owned here

  // ❌ Never do this — props are not yours to change
  // step = 5;

  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + step)}>
      {label}: {count}
    </button>
  );
}
```

---

## The decision procedure

Ask in order:

1. **Does it come from a parent?** → prop
2. **Can I calculate it from existing props or state?** → neither, just compute it during render
3. **Does it stay the same for the component's whole life?** → a plain constant, not state
4. **Otherwise** → state

Step 2 is the one people skip. Derived values do not belong in state:

```jsx
// ❌ Redundant state that can drift out of sync
const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
const [total, setTotal] = useState(0);   // now you must remember to update both

// ✅ Derive it — it cannot be wrong
const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
const total = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
```

---

## The anti-pattern worth naming

Copying a prop into state "so I can change it" silently breaks updates:

```jsx
// ❌ Only reads the prop on first render — later prop changes are ignored
function Profile({ user }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState(user.name);
  return <input value={name} onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

If you genuinely need an editable copy that resets when the source changes, use `key`:

```jsx
<Profile key={user.id} user={user} />   // new id → fresh component, fresh state
```

## The same component, both ways

```jsx
// Class
class Timer extends React.Component {
  state = { seconds: 0 };

  componentDidMount() {
    this.id = setInterval(() => {
      this.setState((s) => ({ seconds: s.seconds + 1 }));
    }, 1000);
  }

  componentWillUnmount() {
    clearInterval(this.id);
  }

  render() {
    return <p>{this.state.seconds}s</p>;
  }
}

// Function
function Timer() {
  const [seconds, setSeconds] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => setSeconds((s) => s + 1), 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);   // setup and teardown side by side
  }, []);

  return <p>{seconds}s</p>;
}
```

---

## Comparison

| | Class | Function |
|---|-------|----------|
| State | `this.state` / `setState` | `useState` |
| Side effects | Lifecycle methods | `useEffect` |
| `this` | Must be bound | Does not exist |
| Logic reuse | HOCs, render props | Custom hooks |
| Code volume | More | Less |
| Future features | Frozen | Where React is investing |

---

## The argument that actually matters

In a class, one concern gets **split across lifecycle methods**: subscribe in `componentDidMount`, resubscribe in `componentDidUpdate`, unsubscribe in `componentWillUnmount`. Meanwhile unrelated concerns get **mixed together** inside each method.

With `useEffect`, one concern is one effect — setup and cleanup in the same block. Multiple effects sit side by side without interleaving.

That is what makes custom hooks possible: you can lift a `useState` + `useEffect` pair into `useWindowSize()` and reuse it. There is no way to extract half a lifecycle method.

---

## What classes can still do

**Error boundaries.** `componentDidCatch` and `getDerivedStateFromError` have no hook equivalent, so error boundaries are still class components — or you use `react-error-boundary`, which wraps a class for you.

---

## The right note to end on

Classes are not deprecated and existing ones do not need rewriting. New code uses functions. "We migrate a component to hooks when we are already touching it" is a mature answer.

## The modern way

```jsx
function Button({ variant = 'primary', size = 'md', disabled = false, children }) {
  return <button className={`btn-${variant} btn-${size}`} disabled={disabled}>{children}</button>;
}
```

Plain JavaScript. No React API involved, works identically in TypeScript, and the defaults are visible right in the signature.

---

## The deprecated way

```jsx
// ❌ Removed for function components in React 19
Button.defaultProps = { variant: 'primary' };
```

Still supported on class components, but there is no reason to use it in new code.

---

## The gotcha: only `undefined` triggers a default

```jsx
function Greeting({ name = 'Guest' }) {
  return <p>Hello, {name}</p>;
}

<Greeting />                  // "Hello, Guest"    ✅
<Greeting name={undefined} />  // "Hello, Guest"    ✅
<Greeting name={null} />       // "Hello, "         ⚠️ null is a value
<Greeting name="" />           // "Hello, "         ⚠️ so is empty string
```

This bites when data comes from an API that returns `null` for missing fields:

```jsx
// ✅ ?? covers null and undefined but preserves 0 and ''
function Greeting({ name }) {
  const display = name ?? 'Guest';
  return <p>Hello, {display}</p>;
}

// ⚠️ || also replaces 0, '' and false — usually wrong for numbers
const count = props.count || 10;   // count={0} silently becomes 10
```

---

## With TypeScript

```tsx
interface ButtonProps {
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
  size?: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg';
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

function Button({ variant = 'primary', size = 'md', children }: ButtonProps) {
  // Inside the body, variant and size are narrowed to non-optional
}
```

The `?` marks the prop optional for callers; the default makes it defined inside. TypeScript understands both, which is another reason `defaultProps` fell out of use — it never typed cleanly.

---

## Defaults for object props

```jsx
// ⚠️ A new object every render — breaks memo and effect dependencies
function Chart({ options = {} }) { }

// ✅ Hoist the constant so the reference is stable
const EMPTY_OPTIONS = {};
function Chart({ options = EMPTY_OPTIONS }) { }
```

## The shape

```jsx
function withAuth(Component) {
  return function Guarded(props) {
    const user = useUser();

    if (!user) return <Redirect to="/login" />;
    return <Component {...props} user={user} />;
  };
}

const ProtectedDashboard = withAuth(Dashboard);
```

It is just a function: component in, component out.

---

## Why hooks displaced them

**Wrapper hell.** HOCs compose by nesting, and the DevTools tree fills with wrappers:

```jsx
export default withRouter(withTheme(withAuth(connect(mapState)(Dashboard))));
// <WithRouter><WithTheme><Guarded><Connect><Dashboard/>...
```

**Prop collisions.** Two HOCs that both inject `data` — the outer one silently loses. Nothing warns you.

**Opaque origins.** Where did `props.user` come from? You have to read every HOC in the chain to find out.

**Typing pain.** Expressing "the returned component takes the original props minus the injected ones" in TypeScript is genuinely awkward.

```jsx
// The hook version — flat, obvious, no collisions
function Dashboard() {
  const user = useUser();
  const theme = useTheme();
  const router = useRouter();
  // The source of every value is right here
}
```

---

## Where HOCs still earn their place

When you need to **render something different** rather than share logic. A hook cannot stop the component from rendering — by the time it runs, you are already inside the component.

| Need | Tool |
|------|------|
| Share stateful logic | Custom hook |
| Render a fallback instead | HOC or a wrapper component |
| Inject props into a third-party component | HOC |
| Wrap with an error boundary | HOC or wrapper |

In practice a plain wrapper component is usually clearer than an HOC for these:

```jsx
// Often preferred over withAuth()
<RequireAuth>
  <Dashboard />
</RequireAuth>
```

---

## Rules if you write one

1. Pass through unrelated props with `{...props}`
2. Set `displayName` so DevTools is readable
3. Copy static methods (or use `hoist-non-react-statics`)
4. Never call it inside render — `withAuth(Dashboard)` at render time creates a new component type every render and remounts the whole subtree

## The problem it fixes

Prop explosion:

```jsx
// A component slowly turning into a configuration language
<Accordion
  items={items}
  renderHeader={...}
  renderIcon={...}
  headerClassName="..."
  iconPosition="right"
  allowMultiple
  defaultOpenIndex={0}
/>
```

Every new design requirement adds a prop. The component ends up knowing about layout decisions it should not care about.

---

## The compound version

```jsx
<Accordion defaultOpen="billing">
  <Accordion.Item value="billing">
    <Accordion.Trigger>Billing <ChevronIcon /></Accordion.Trigger>
    <Accordion.Panel>Your invoices…</Accordion.Panel>
  </Accordion.Item>

  <Accordion.Item value="security">
    <Accordion.Trigger>Security</Accordion.Trigger>
    <Accordion.Panel>Two-factor auth…</Accordion.Panel>
  </Accordion.Item>
</Accordion>
```

Structure is now the caller's business. `Accordion` only owns "which item is open".

---

## How it is built

```jsx
const AccordionContext = createContext(null);

function Accordion({ defaultOpen, children }) {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(defaultOpen);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ open, setOpen }), [open]);

  return (
    <AccordionContext.Provider value={value}>
      <div className="accordion">{children}</div>
    </AccordionContext.Provider>
  );
}

function useAccordion() {
  const ctx = useContext(AccordionContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error('Accordion.* must be used inside <Accordion>');
  return ctx;
}

Accordion.Trigger = function Trigger({ children }) {
  const { open, setOpen } = useAccordion();
  const { value } = useItem();
  return (
    <button aria-expanded={open === value} onClick={() => setOpen(value)}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
};
```

That throwing `useAccordion` is important — it turns "used outside the provider" from a confusing null error into a clear message.

---

## Trade-offs

| | Compound | Props-based |
|---|---|---|
| Flexibility | High | Low |
| Call-site verbosity | Higher | Lower |
| Discoverability | Weaker — you must read the docs | Autocomplete shows every prop |
| Best for | Design systems, reused widgets | One-off internal components |

Do not reach for this on a component used in one place. It pays off when many teams consume the same widget and each wants a slightly different arrangement — which is exactly why Radix UI, Headless UI and React Aria are all built this way.

## What it looked like

```jsx
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

function User({ name, age, tags }) { /* … */ }

User.propTypes = {
  name: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
  age: PropTypes.number,
  tags: PropTypes.arrayOf(PropTypes.string),
};
```

Pass the wrong type and you get a console warning **in development only** — the component still renders.

---

## Why TypeScript replaced it

| | PropTypes | TypeScript |
|---|-----------|------------|
| When errors appear | Runtime, after you click the thing | Compile time, in the editor |
| Coverage | Props only | Everything |
| Editor support | None | Autocomplete, rename, go-to-definition |
| Production cost | Stripped, but the definitions ship | Zero — erased at build |
| React 19 | **Removed** | Fully supported |

```tsx
interface UserProps {
  name: string;
  age?: number;
  tags?: string[];
}

function User({ name, age, tags }: UserProps) { /* … */ }
```

The error now appears as you type, before the code runs.

---

## The gap TypeScript leaves

TypeScript checks your code. It does **not** check data crossing a runtime boundary:

```tsx
// TypeScript believes you. The API may disagree.
const user = await res.json() as User;
```

That is where **Zod** or **Valibot** come in — runtime validation that also produces the static type:

```tsx
import { z } from 'zod';

const UserSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string(),
  age: z.number().optional(),
});

type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;   // static type, derived
const user = UserSchema.parse(await res.json());   // runtime check, throws if wrong
```

---

## How to answer

"PropTypes was runtime prop validation, removed in React 19. We use TypeScript for static checking and Zod at the API boundary, because TypeScript cannot validate data it did not create." That covers the history and shows you understand where static typing actually stops.

## Real signals to split

**1. More than one responsibility.** If you would describe it as "it fetches the user *and* renders the form *and* handles submission", those are three things.

**2. Reuse.** The moment you copy a block into a second file, extract it.

**3. Re-render scope.** State that only affects a subtree should live in that subtree:

```jsx
// ❌ Typing in the search box re-renders the entire expensive table
function Page() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveTable />
      <Results query={query} />
    </>
  );
}

// ✅ State pushed down — the table no longer participates
function Page() {
  return (
    <>
      <SearchAndResults />   {/* owns `query` */}
      <ExpensiveTable />
    </>
  );
}
```

**4. Deep nesting.** Three levels of nested ternaries or map-inside-map is usually a missing component.

**5. A name suggests itself.** If you can name the block confidently — `<PriceBreakdown>`, `<AvatarStack>` — it wants to exist.

---

## Bad reasons to split

| Reason | Why it is wrong |
|--------|-----------------|
| "The file is over 200 lines" | Length is a symptom, not the disease |
| "One component per file is the rule" | Small helpers used once belong beside their user |
| "It might be reused someday" | Speculative abstraction usually guesses wrong |

---

## The cost of over-splitting

Ten files each 15 lines long is not obviously better than one 150-line file. You have traded scrolling for jumping between files, and a change now touches five of them. Props multiply to thread data through the extra layers, and nothing is easier to follow.

---

## A practical heuristic

> Can you describe what the component does in one sentence without using "and"?

"Renders a product card" — fine. "Fetches the product and renders a card and tracks analytics" — three components, or one component plus two custom hooks.

Extracting logic into a **custom hook** is often the better move. It separates concerns without adding a layer to the component tree.

## The standard form

```tsx
interface CardProps {
  title: string;
  subtitle?: string;
  onDismiss?: () => void;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

function Card({ title, subtitle, onDismiss, children }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      {subtitle && <p>{subtitle}</p>}
      {children}
    </div>
  );
}
```

---

## Why not `React.FC`

```tsx
// Common in older code
const Card: React.FC<CardProps> = ({ title }) => { … };
```

Before React 18 it silently added `children` to every component, even ones that should not accept it. That is fixed, but it still gives you nothing the plain annotation does not, and it makes generic components awkward. The community settled on annotating the parameter.

---

## Inheriting native props

```tsx
// Every valid <button> attribute, plus your own
interface ButtonProps extends React.ComponentPropsWithoutRef<'button'> {
  variant?: 'primary' | 'danger';
}

function Button({ variant = 'primary', ...rest }: ButtonProps) {
  return <button className={`btn-${variant}`} {...rest} />;
}
```

Use `ComponentPropsWithRef<'button'>` instead if the component forwards a ref.

---

## The types you will reach for

| Type | For |
|------|-----|
| `React.ReactNode` | Anything renderable — the right type for `children` |
| `React.ReactElement` | Specifically a JSX element, not strings or null |
| `React.CSSProperties` | A `style` object |
| `React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>` | `onChange` on an input |
| `React.MouseEvent<HTMLButtonElement>` | A click handler |
| `React.Dispatch<React.SetStateAction<T>>` | Passing a `setState` function down |

---

## Unions beat booleans

```tsx
// ❌ Allows the impossible combination of both true
interface Props { isLoading?: boolean; isError?: boolean; }

// ✅ Only valid states are representable
type Props =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'error'; error: Error }
  | { status: 'success'; data: User };
```

A discriminated union lets TypeScript narrow inside each branch, so `error` is only accessible when `status` is `'error'`. Making invalid states unrepresentable is the single highest-value TypeScript habit in React code.

## The problem

```jsx
// Two independent copies of "the temperature" — they will disagree
function Celsius()    { const [c, setC] = useState(0); … }
function Fahrenheit() { const [f, setF] = useState(32); … }
```

Type in one and the other is now wrong. There is no way to keep them in sync from inside either.

---

## Lifting it

```jsx
function Converter() {
  const [celsius, setCelsius] = useState(0);   // one source of truth

  return (
    <>
      <TempInput
        label="Celsius"
        value={celsius}
        onChange={setCelsius}
      />
      <TempInput
        label="Fahrenheit"
        value={celsius * 9 / 5 + 32}                  // derived, never stored
        onChange={(f) => setCelsius((f - 32) * 5 / 9)}
      />
    </>
  );
}

function TempInput({ label, value, onChange }) {
  return (
    <label>
      {label}
      <input value={value} onChange={(e) => onChange(Number(e.target.value))} />
    </label>
  );
}
```

`TempInput` is now fully controlled — it owns nothing and cannot drift.

---

## The rule

> State lives at the **closest common ancestor** of every component that reads or writes it.

Not the root. Not `App`. The closest one.

---

## The cost

Every lift widens the re-render blast radius. State in `App` means the whole tree re-renders on every change. That is why "put everything in a global store" is not the free answer it looks like.

The opposite move — **pushing state down** into the smallest component that needs it — is a real optimisation technique and usually the first thing to try when a page feels sluggish.

---

## When lifting stops being right

If you find yourself lifting through five levels, the value is not really local state any more. At that point:

- **Composition** — pass the consumer as `children` so no lifting is needed
- **Context** — for genuinely ambient values like theme or current user
- **A store** — Zustand, Jotai or Redux, when many distant components read and write it

Lifting is the right default. It only becomes wrong when the distance gets long.
